# Only the options below and the sections between "Thoughts for when there is programming involved" and "After making changes" are this project's own; every other section is overwritten.
{{ if options_text }}

{{ options_text }}
{{ /if }}
-->

# Environment

Welcome. You're on a NixOS {{ nixos_version }} machine where many things are already installed, including:

{{ tools.join(", ") }}.

Before starting or resuming work, check what `hostname` outputs.

- If it ends in "clank", {{ sandbox }}run whatever commands you need.
- If any other hostname, stop and ask the user to edit this file.
{{ if extra.environment }}

{{ extra.environment }}
{{ /if }}

# Avoid consuming tokens in excess

When verifying how something works, use e.g. `rg -B2 -A10` until you need the whole file.

# The user isn't always right

{{ include "push_back.vto" }}

Please don't assume the user really wants all the things that already exist; this isn't always the case, as sometimes there are odd leftovers.

Always let the user know about opportunities for simplification.

If the user asks for more than one change, try doing and committing them separately (unless the changes are entwined).
{{ if node }}

# Working with Node projects

npm sux; please use pnpm instead.
{{ /if }}

# There's plenty of time

If more external information is needed, think and keep iterating on web search queries to thoroughly check things. Tips: try site-specific searches e.g. site:github.com, reddit.com, news.ycombinator.com; try combinations of quoted items.

If you can't fetch something, use google-chrome or {{ curl_chrome }} on this machine.

# Tracking AI authorship

Files with any LLM-authored code (not counting mechanistic sed-like changes) begin with a comment like `// Model-output: <model name>`, one per model that contributed (e.g. "{{ claude_model }}", "{{ chatgpt_model }}"); keep existing lines.

# Code conventions
{{ include "code_conventions.vto" }}
{{ if extra.code_conventions }}

{{ extra.code_conventions }}
{{ /if }}
{{ if node }}

# Libraries to use

- `ayy` to assert things (when it's okay to throw `AssertionError` instead of a domain-specific error).
  - Always use the most specific assertion function, so that the error message shows the unexpected value when possible.
    - e.g. A.gte(x, 1); A.lte(x, 3) instead of A(x >= 1 && x <= 3);
- `logtape` for logging. Logs teach us about anomalies and the causes of things; log what a human operator would probably be interested in when observing the system.
{{ if effection }}
- `effection`. We like structured programming and async/await is lacking; prefer to write an Operation instead of a Promise-returning function (unless needed).
{{ /if }}
{{ for library of libraries_extra }}
- {{ library }}
{{ /for }}
- `vitest` for unit tests. Writing more tests is fine.
- `fast-check` for property-based testing where needed, i.e. to check a bunch of variations on a string or number.
{{ /if }}
{{ if web_design == "2010" }}

# Web design

Set your mind to 2010 styling and information density; rely almost entirely on lightly styled (not referring to color) native HTML elements.

{{ if color_scheme == "dark" }}
This is a "dark mode"-only project, so text should be light and backgrounds should be dark.
{{ else if color_scheme == "light" }}
This is a "light mode"-only project, so text should be black and backgrounds should be light.
{{ else }}
Please keep things working well in both light mode and dark mode.
{{ /if }}

Write CSS mostly the old way and don't add more than you really need.

It's okay to make things look nice within these constraints, like using 8px padding or grid, suitable line-height, color or font-weight or font-family for differentiation, etc.

We really want things to be obvious at a glance while not having superfluous stuff.

Also, no new title= tooltips; they're dubious desktop-only things which must be written by humans.
